IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `types`
IMPORT `chapter-4-exceptions`

§ `Chapter 4A -- Right of private defence`

-- Penal Code 1871 ss 96, 97, 98, 99, 100, 101, 102, 103, 104, 105, 106, 106A.
-- Source: registers/source-bundle/PC1871.txt.
--
-- s 6 makes every definition of an offence and every penal provision subject to the
-- Chapter entitled "Right of Private Defence" as well as to the Chapter 4 general
-- exceptions. `chapter-4-exceptions.l4` carries only the first of those two. This
-- module carries the second, and `the act is taken outside offence by Chapter 4 or
-- Chapter 4A` at the foot of this file is the predicate the offence wrappers use.
--
-- Shape of the Chapter. Two rights -- of the body (ss 97(a), 101, 102, 103) and of
-- property (ss 97(b), 104, 105, 106) -- each with the same four-part structure:
-- a subject matter, a window in which the right runs, a general restriction, and a
-- ceiling on the harm that may be inflicted. ss 98 and 106A restrict both. s 99
-- feeds both. s 100 extends the body right alone. s 96 states the consequence.

§§ `Section 98 -- Extent to which the right may be exercised`

-- s 98(1): the right does not extend to the inflicting of more harm than it is
-- reasonably necessary in the circumstances.
-- s 98(2): there is no right of private defence in cases in which there is
-- reasonable opportunity to have recourse to the protection of a public authority
-- in the circumstances. Illustration (b) makes the timing point: the right ceases
-- the moment that opportunity arises, mid-struggle if need be.
-- Both subsections restrict every exercise of the right, of the body or of
-- property, so they are gathered here and conjoined by each right below.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 98"
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a section 98 restriction defeats the right` IF
        pd's `inflicts more harm than it is reasonably necessary in the circumstances`
    OR  pd's `there is reasonable opportunity to have recourse to the protection of a public authority in the circumstances`

§§ `Section 106A -- Acts against which there is no right of private defence`

-- s 106A(1): there is no right of private defence against an act which does not
-- cause the defender to reasonably believe that death or grievous hurt would result,
-- if done, or attempted to be done, by a public servant acting in good faith under
-- the actual or apparent authority of his office, though that act may not be
-- strictly justifiable by law.
-- s 106A(2): the same, for an act done by the direction of such a public servant.
--
-- Note the double negative. The bar bites only where the act does NOT cause the
-- reasonable belief of death or grievous hurt; where it does, the right survives
-- even against a public servant. So the first conjunct of each limb is a negation.
--
-- Explanation 1: a person is not deprived of the right unless he knows, or has
-- reason to believe, that the person doing the act is such a public servant.
-- Explanation 2: on the direction limb he is not deprived unless he knows or has
-- reason to believe that the person is acting by such direction, or unless that
-- person states the authority under which he acts or, having authority in writing,
-- produces it if demanded. Those are alternatives, so they are disjoined.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 106A(1)"
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 106A(1) removes the right` IF
        (NOT pd's `the act causes the defender to reasonably believe that death or grievous hurt would result`)
    AND pd's `the act defended against is done or attempted by a public servant acting in good faith under the actual or apparent authority of his office`
    AND pd's `the defender knows or has reason to believe that the person doing the act is such a public servant`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 106A(2)"
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 106A(2) removes the right` IF
        (NOT pd's `the act causes the defender to reasonably believe that death or grievous hurt would result`)
    AND pd's `the act defended against is done or attempted by the direction of a public servant acting in good faith under the actual or apparent authority of that public servant office`
    AND (   pd's `the defender knows or has reason to believe that the person doing the act is acting by such direction`
         OR pd's `the person doing the act states the authority under which he acts or produces his written authority if demanded`)

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 106A"
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 106A removes the right` IF
        `section 106A(1) removes the right` pd
    OR  `section 106A(2) removes the right` pd

§§ `Section 99 -- Right of private defence against the act of a person of unsound mind, etc.`

-- When an act which would otherwise be a certain offence is not that offence, by
-- reason of the youth, the want of maturity of understanding, the unsoundness of
-- mind or the intoxication of the person doing it, or by reason of any misconception
-- on his part, every person has the same right of private defence against that act
-- which he would have if the act were that offence.
--
-- s 99 is therefore not a right of its own. It is a second route to the "is an
-- offence" element that ss 97, 101 and 104 each require, and it is encoded as an
-- alternative limb inside those tests rather than as a limb of s 96.
-- Illustration (a): Z attacks A under the influence of madness and is guilty of no
-- offence; A has the same right of private defence as if Z were sane.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 99"
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 99 supplies the offence element` IF
    pd's `the act defended against would be that offence but for the youth want of maturity of understanding unsoundness of mind or intoxication of the person doing it or a misconception on his part`

§§ `Section 97 -- Right of private defence of the body and of property`

-- Every person has a right, subject to the restrictions in ss 98 and 106A, to defend
-- (a) his own body, and the body of any other person, against any offence affecting
-- the human body; and (b) the property, whether movable or immovable, of himself or
-- of any other person, against any act which is an offence falling under the
-- definition of theft, robbery, mischief or criminal trespass, or which is an
-- attempt to commit one of them.
--
-- The ss 98 and 106A restrictions are not conjoined here. s 97 states what the right
-- covers; the restrictions are applied once, in the availability tests below, so
-- that they bite on both rights in one place.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 97(a)"
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `there is a section 97(a) right to defend the body` IF
        pd's `defends his own body or the body of any other person`
    AND (   pd's `the act defended against is an offence affecting the human body`
         OR `section 99 supplies the offence element` pd)

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 97(b)"
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `there is a section 97(b) right to defend property` IF
        pd's `defends property whether movable or immovable of himself or of any other person`
    AND (   pd's `the act defended against is theft robbery mischief or criminal trespass or an attempt to commit one of them`
         OR `section 99 supplies the offence element` pd)

§§ `Section 101 -- Start and continuance of the right of private defence of body`

-- s 101(1): the right starts as soon as the defender reasonably believes that there
-- is danger to the body (his own or that of any other person) arising from any act
-- which is an offence against the human body or an attempt or a threat to commit the
-- offence, though the offence may not have been committed.
-- s 101(2): it continues as long as that belief of danger continues.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 101"
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the right of private defence of the body has started and continues` IF
        pd's `the defender reasonably believes there is danger to the body of himself or any other person arising from an act which is an offence against the human body or an attempt or a threat to commit it`
    AND pd's `that belief of danger continues`

§§ `Section 100 -- Deadly assault where there is risk of harm to an innocent person`

-- If, in the exercise of the right of private defence against an assault which
-- causes the defender to reasonably believe that death would be caused to him or to
-- any other person, the defender is so situated that he cannot effectually exercise
-- that right without risk of harm to an innocent person, his right extends to the
-- running of that risk.
--
-- s 100 enlarges the right rather than restricting it, so it is encoded as a
-- permission that is needed only when harm to an innocent person is actually
-- risked. The extent test below reads: either no innocent person is harmed, or
-- s 100 covers it.
--
-- CALLER NOTE. s 100's opening fact and s 102(a) are near neighbours: s 100 speaks
-- of an assault causing the defender to reasonably believe that death would be
-- caused to him or to any other person, and s 102(a) of an assault where the
-- defender reasonably believes death will otherwise be the consequence. They are
-- kept as two facts because the two sections word them differently and do different
-- work -- s 100 licenses the risk to a bystander, s 102(a) lifts the ceiling on
-- harm to the assailant. On facts where both are true, a caller must set both.
-- Setting only one is safe in the sense that it can only withhold the
-- justification, never invent one.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 100"
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 100 extends the right to the risk of harm to an innocent person` IF
        pd's `the assault causes the defender to reasonably believe that death would be caused to him or to any other person`
    AND pd's `the defender cannot effectually exercise the right without risk of harm to an innocent person`

§§ `Section 102 -- When the right of private defence of body extends to causing death`

-- The right extends, under the restrictions in ss 98 and 106A, to the voluntary
-- causing of death to the assailant if the offence which gives rise to the exercise
-- of the right is of any of six descriptions: (a) an assault where the defender
-- reasonably believes death will otherwise be the consequence; (b) the same as to
-- grievous hurt; (c) an assault the defender reasonably believes to be done with the
-- intention of committing rape as described in s 375 or of causing such rape to be
-- committed; (d) with the intention of causing penile penetration as described in
-- s 376(2); (e) with the intention of kidnapping or abducting; (f) with the
-- intention of wrongfully confining a person, in circumstances which may reasonably
-- cause him to believe that he will be unable to have recourse to a public authority
-- for his release.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 102"
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the offence is of a section 102 description` IF
        pd's `the defender reasonably believes death will otherwise be the consequence of the assault`
    OR  pd's `the defender reasonably believes grievous hurt will otherwise be the consequence of the assault`
    OR  pd's `the defender reasonably believes the assault is done with the intention of committing rape as described in section 375 or of causing such rape to be committed`
    OR  pd's `the defender reasonably believes the assault is done with the intention of causing penile penetration as described in section 376(2)`
    OR  pd's `the defender reasonably believes the assault is done with the intention of kidnapping or abducting`
    OR  pd's `the defender reasonably believes the assault is done with the intention of wrongfully confining a person in circumstances reasonably causing him to believe he will be unable to have recourse to a public authority for his release`

§§ `Section 103 -- When the body right extends to harm other than death`

-- If the offence is not of any of the s 102 descriptions, the right does not extend
-- to the voluntary causing of death to the assailant, but does extend, under the
-- restrictions in ss 98 and 106A, to the voluntary causing of any harm other than
-- death.
--
-- ss 102 and 103 together fix a ceiling, not a further element, so they are encoded
-- as one test on the harm the defender actually caused: death is within the right
-- only on a s 102 description, and any lesser harm is within it either way.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, ss 102 and 103"
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the harm caused is within the extent of the body right` IF
        (   (NOT pd's `the defender voluntarily causes death to the assailant or wrongdoer`)
         OR `the offence is of a section 102 description` pd)
    AND (   (NOT pd's `harm is caused to an innocent person in the exercise of the right`)
         OR `section 100 extends the right to the risk of harm to an innocent person` pd)

§§ `The right of private defence of the body, made out`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, ss 97, 98, 101, 106A"
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the right of private defence of the body is available` IF
        `there is a section 97(a) right to defend the body` pd
    AND `the right of private defence of the body has started and continues` pd
    AND (NOT `a section 98 restriction defeats the right` pd)
    AND (NOT `section 106A removes the right` pd)

@ref "Penal Code 1871, ss 97 to 103"
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `private defence of the body justifies the act` IF
        `the right of private defence of the body is available` pd
    AND `the harm caused is within the extent of the body right` pd

§§ `Section 104 -- Commencement and continuance of the right of private defence of property`

-- s 104(1): the right starts when the defender reasonably believes that there was a
-- danger to property (his own or that of any other person) arising from any act
-- which is an offence falling under the definition of theft, robbery, mischief or
-- criminal trespass, or which is an attempt to commit such an offence.
-- s 104(2): against theft it continues till the offender has effected his retreat
-- with the property, or the assistance of a public authority is obtained, or the
-- property has been recovered -- so each of those three ENDS the right.
-- s 104(3): against robbery, as long as the offender causes or attempts to cause
-- death or hurt or wrongful restraint, or as long as the fear of instant death or of
-- instant hurt or of instant personal restraint continues.
-- s 104(4): against criminal trespass or mischief, as long as the offender continues
-- in the commission of it.
-- s 104(5): against house-breaking, as long as the house-breaking continues.
--
-- The four continuance rules are keyed to the offence defended against, so the test
-- is a disjunction over the four offence kinds rather than four separate rules: on
-- any given facts exactly one limb is live.
--
-- Note the asymmetry in the Act's own lists. s 104(1) starts the right against
-- theft, robbery, mischief or criminal trespass; s 104(5) continues it against
-- house-breaking, which s 104(1) does not name. House-breaking is an aggravated form
-- of criminal trespass (ss 442 to 449), so it falls within the s 104(1) list, and a
-- caller asserting the house-breaking limb must assert the s 104(1) belief as well.
-- Neither ss 442 to 449 nor any other Chapter 17 trespass section is encoded here,
-- so that containment is stated rather than computed.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 104(1)"
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the right of private defence of property has started` IF
    pd's `the defender reasonably believes there was a danger to property of himself or any other person arising from an act which is theft robbery mischief or criminal trespass or an attempt to commit such an offence`

-- CALLER NOTE. Every offence s 104(1) starts the right against has a continuance
-- rule in s 104(2) to (5), so this test is FALSE until one of the four offence-kind
-- facts is set. A caller who asserts the s 104(1) belief and no offence kind gets no
-- property right, which is the conservative direction.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 104(2) to (5)"
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the right of private defence of property continues` IF
        (    pd's `the offence defended against is theft`
         AND (NOT pd's `the offender has effected his retreat with the property`)
         AND (NOT pd's `the assistance of a public authority is obtained`)
         AND (NOT pd's `the property has been recovered`))
    OR  (    pd's `the offence defended against is robbery`
         AND pd's `the offender causes or attempts to cause death or hurt or wrongful restraint or the fear of instant death or instant hurt or instant personal restraint continues`)
    OR  (    pd's `the offence defended against is criminal trespass or mischief`
         AND pd's `the offender continues in the commission of the criminal trespass or mischief`)
    OR  (    pd's `the offence defended against is house-breaking`
         AND pd's `the house-breaking continues`)

§§ `Section 105 -- When the right of private defence of property extends to causing death`

-- s 105(1): the right extends, under the restrictions in ss 98 and 106A, to the
-- voluntary causing of death to the wrongdoer where the defender reasonably believes
-- that there was a danger to property arising from any of four descriptions:
-- (a) robbery; (b) house-breaking committed after 7 p.m. and before 7 a.m. where the
-- wrongdoer enters or leaves in any of the 6 ways in s 105(2); (c) mischief by fire
-- on a building, tent, container or vessel used as a human dwelling or as a place
-- for the custody of property; (d) theft, mischief or house-breaking in
-- circumstances where the defender reasonably believes death or grievous hurt will
-- be the consequence if the right is not exercised.
-- The s 105(1) opening belief is the same fact as the s 104(1) one, and is not
-- restated: it is already required for the right to have started at all.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 105(2)"
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the wrongdoer entered or left in one of the 6 ways in section 105(2)` IF
        pd's `the wrongdoer enters or leaves through a passage made by the wrongdoer or by an abettor of the house-breaking in order to commit it`
    OR  pd's `the wrongdoer enters or leaves through a passage not intended by any other person for human entrance or to which access was obtained by scaling or climbing over a wall or building`
    OR  pd's `the wrongdoer enters or leaves through a passage opened in order to commit the house-breaking by means by which the occupier did not intend it to be opened`
    OR  pd's `the wrongdoer enters or leaves by opening any lock in order to commit the house-breaking or to leave the house after one`
    OR  pd's `the wrongdoer effects his entry or leaving by using criminal force or committing an assault or by threatening any person with assault`
    OR  pd's `the wrongdoer enters or leaves by a passage he knows to have been fastened against such entry or leaving and to have been unfastened by himself or by an abettor`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 105(1)"
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the danger is of a section 105 description` IF
        pd's `the offence defended against is robbery`
    OR  (    pd's `the offence defended against is house-breaking`
         AND pd's `the house-breaking was committed after 7 p.m. and before 7 a.m.`
         AND `the wrongdoer entered or left in one of the 6 ways in section 105(2)` pd)
    OR  pd's `the danger is mischief by fire on a building tent container or vessel used as a human dwelling or as a place for the custody of property`
    OR  pd's `the danger is theft mischief or house-breaking in circumstances where the defender reasonably believes death or grievous hurt will be the consequence if the right is not exercised`

§§ `Section 106 -- When the property right extends to harm other than death`

-- If the offence occasioning the exercise of the right is theft, mischief or
-- criminal trespass not of any of the s 105 descriptions, the right does not extend
-- to the voluntary causing of death, but does extend, subject to the restrictions in
-- ss 98 and 106A, to the voluntary causing of any harm other than death.
-- As with ss 102 and 103, the pair fixes a ceiling on the harm, not a further
-- element of the right.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, ss 105 and 106"
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the harm caused is within the extent of the property right` IF
        (NOT pd's `the defender voluntarily causes death to the assailant or wrongdoer`)
    OR  `the danger is of a section 105 description` pd

§§ `The right of private defence of property, made out`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, ss 97, 98, 104, 106A"
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the right of private defence of property is available` IF
        `there is a section 97(b) right to defend property` pd
    AND `the right of private defence of property has started` pd
    AND `the right of private defence of property continues` pd
    AND (NOT `a section 98 restriction defeats the right` pd)
    AND (NOT `section 106A removes the right` pd)

@ref "Penal Code 1871, ss 97 and 104 to 106"
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `private defence of property justifies the act` IF
        `the right of private defence of property is available` pd
    AND `the harm caused is within the extent of the property right` pd

§§ `Section 96 -- Nothing done in private defence is an offence`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 96"
@export Decide whether the right of private defence under Penal Code 1871 Chapter 4A means the act is not an offence
GIVEN pd IS A `Private Defence Facts` @desc The private-defence facts. Set only the flags true of the act; the rest stay false. Both rights need a subject matter (s 97), a live window (s 101 for the body, s 104 for property), no s 98 restriction and no s 106A bar; causing death additionally needs a s 102 or s 105 description.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a private defence justification is made out` IF
        `private defence of the body justifies the act` pd
    OR  `private defence of property justifies the act` pd

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 96"
@export Decide whether the proposed act is done in the exercise of the right of private defence under Penal Code 1871 Chapter 4A
GIVEN a IS A `Proposed Act` @desc The proposed act. Its private defence facts carry the whole of Chapter 4A.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a private defence justification applies` IF
    `a private defence justification is made out` (a's `private defence facts`)

§§ `Section 6 -- Chapter 4 and Chapter 4A together`

-- s 6: every definition of an offence and every penal provision is understood
-- subject to the exceptions contained in the Chapters entitled "General Exceptions"
-- AND "Right of Private Defence". The offence wrappers in this encoding call this
-- predicate rather than `a general exception applies`, so that a private-defence
-- justification takes the act outside the offence exactly as a Chapter 4 exception
-- does.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 6"
@export Decide whether Penal Code 1871 section 6 takes the proposed act outside offence, by a Chapter 4 general exception or a Chapter 4A private defence
GIVEN a IS A `Proposed Act` @desc The proposed act. Its exception facts carry Chapter 4 and its private defence facts carry Chapter 4A.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the act is taken outside offence by Chapter 4 or Chapter 4A` IF
        `a general exception applies` a
    OR  `a private defence justification applies` a
